└── finite state machine engine for modeling controller states/transitions
    ├── Candidates : transitions, fysom, python-statemachine
    ├── Used APIs  : Machine, add_transition, conditions=, after=, model=self, wildcard '*' source state, self.state
    ├── Pitfalls   : transitions binds trigger methods (start_fill, level_reached, etc.) dynamically onto self via model=self;
    │   fysom and python-statemachine do not support this exact dynamic-attachment pattern and require restructuring how 
    │   transition-trigger methods are invoked; wildcard source state '*' (used for the 'trip' transition) is a 
    │   transitions-specific convenience; equivalent behavior must be manually reconstructed by listing all states in the 
    │   alternative library; python-statemachine requires transitions to be declared as class-level State()/Transition() 
    │   descriptors, meaning the whole TankController class structure would need to change, not just the import line; 
    │   conditions= and after= callback wiring syntax differs between all three libraries and callback names must be resolved 
    │   against class methods differently; state representation (self.state) semantics and equality checks may differ subtly 
    │   between libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── transitions is used here as a generic FSM engine bound dynamically to an arbitrary model object (model=self), with 
        declarative add_transition calls supporting guard conditions, after-callbacks, and a wildcard '*' source state for the
        'trip' transition. fysom (the library transitions was originally inspired by) offers a comparable declarative 
        event/transition API with condition support and could serve a similar role. python-statemachine offers similar 
        conceptual coverage (states, transitions, guards, callbacks) but via a class-based declarative style rather than 
        dynamic model binding.

