  ⚠ Remaining incompatibilities noted by connector LLM:
    - minimalmodbus is fundamentally a serial (RTU/ASCII over pyserial) Modbus master library and has no Modbus-TCP transport;
ModbusTcpClient(host, port) has no counterpart and cannot be constructed from host/port arguments alone.
    - client.connect() has no minimalmodbus equivalent — minimalmodbus opens the serial port implicitly on Instrument 
construction; there is no separate connect/disconnect lifecycle to map pymodbus's explicit connect() call onto for a 
TCP-oriented design.
    - client.read_holding_registers(address, count, unit=self.unit) returns a response object with .isError() and .registers 
in pymodbus; minimalmodbus's read_registers(registeraddress, numberOfRegisters, functioncode) returns a plain list of ints 
directly and raises exceptions on error instead of returning an error-flagged object — while a wrapper could reshape this 
return value, it still requires an Instrument bound to a fixed slave address at construction time, not a per-call 'unit' 
parameter, which conflicts with the existing per-call unit-based design and the earlier TCP transport gap.
    - client.write_register(address, counts, unit=self.unit) similarly returns a response object with .isError() in pymodbus, 
while minimalmodbus's write_register returns None/raises on error and has no per-call 'unit' argument (slave id is fixed per 
Instrument) — again dependent on there being a working Instrument, which cannot be created for a TCP host in the first place.
    - Overall: the code's dependency-injected design assumes a Modbus TCP client at a host:port; minimalmodbus provides no 
TCP/socket-based master implementation whatsoever, so this is a structural transport gap that no amount of wrapper/glue code 
can bridge.
